    "lawCode": "Amd §11-0536, En Con L",
    "lawSection": "Environmental Conservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5766\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the environmental conservation law, in relation to the\nsale of certain wild animals\n\n\n PURPOSE :\nTo continue the prohibition on the sale of the endangered Tasmanian\nForester Kangaroo, including skin and body parts thereof, and to\npermit non endangered species to be sold.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\nSection 1 of the bill would modify the prohibition on the sale of\nkangaroos and amend Environmental Conservation Law § 11-0536 (1) to\nprohibit the sale of the endangered Tasmanian Forester Kangaroo\n(Macropus giganteus tasmaniensis).\n\n EXISTING LAW :\nEnvironmental Conservation law § 11-0536 (1) currently prohibits the\nsale or offer for sale of wild animals or products from wild animals\nfor listed species\n\nState regulations (6 NYCRR 182.3) prohibits the taking, importation,\ntransportation, possession or sale of any species of fish or wildlife,\nor part thereof, designated as endangered or threatened by the\nSecretary of the Interior or the Department of Environmental\nConservation.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThe Tasmanian Forester Kangaroo (Macropus giganteus tasmaniensis) is\nlisted as an endangered kangaroo subspecies by the United States\nDepartment of the Interior's Fish and Wildlife Service pursuant to the\nFederal Endangered Species of 1973 (as amended 16 U.S.C. 1531 et\nseq.). The Tasmanian Forester Kangaroo was listed as endangered on\nJune 4, 1973 due to over harvesting dating from the 1800s and habitat\nloss resulting from agricultural activity. Currently the sales\nprohibition applies to all kangaroos although no other subspecies of\nkangaroo are listed, or are candidates for listing, as endangered or\nthreatened by the federal government.\n\nWhile current New York statute prohibits the sale of kangaroo, state\nregulations prohibit the possession and sale of any species of fish\nand wildlife listed as endangered or threatened pursuant to the\nfederal Endangered Species Act. As a result, there is an ambiguity in\nthe law as to whether the prohibition on the sale or possession of\nKangaroo applies to the entire species or is reasonably intended to\nprotect only that subspecies designated for such protection - the\nendangered Tasmanian Forester Kangaroo.\n\nThis bill would resolve this ambiguity to make it clear that the sale\nor possession of the endangered Tasmanian Forester Kangaroo is\nprohibited and that the state's enforcement and regulatory resources\nshould appropriately be directed to its protection.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the 90th day after enactment.\n",
